WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.440
Usually when we talk about getting a medical diagnosis, there's this expectation of absolute precision, right?

00:06.520 --> 00:13.060
Like you go to the doctor, you break your arm, the x-ray shows that jagged white line in the bone, and the doctor just points at it.

00:13.100 --> 00:17.300
Exactly. They say, there it is. It's an observable, physical thing.

00:17.560 --> 00:21.540
Right. It is a structural reality. The bone is broken or it is not broken.

00:21.540 --> 00:34.560
But when you step into the world of artificial intelligence, specifically how these massive language models supposedly, you know, remember or know things, well, that x-ray machine is completely useless.

00:34.820 --> 00:38.540
Yeah. We are suddenly looking at a diagnostic landscape that is entirely opaque.

00:38.800 --> 00:45.080
Completely opaque. Because think about it. If an AI gives you the right answer to a factual question on Tuesday, right?

00:45.460 --> 00:51.120
And then on Wednesday, it completely fabricates an answer to the exact same question, just phrased a little different.

00:51.120 --> 00:52.620
I mean, what actually happened there?

00:52.860 --> 00:57.220
Did the data get corrupted? Did the system somehow just forget?

00:57.680 --> 01:05.280
Yeah, that's the mystery. And today we are going to challenge the very foundational vocabulary we use to talk about AI memory.

01:05.940 --> 01:16.360
So welcome to the Deep Dive. Whether you are prepping for a big tech meeting or you're just, you know, insanely curious about how these artificial brains actually work, we've got something fascinating for you.

01:16.360 --> 01:20.980
We really do. We are looking at this incredibly dense, independent research paper by Flyxion.

01:21.420 --> 01:28.160
It's titled Access Before Storage, a continuation ontology reading of parametric factuality.

01:28.280 --> 01:30.040
A very academic title, I know.

01:30.220 --> 01:30.400
Yeah.

01:30.520 --> 01:31.860
But OK, let's unpack this.

01:31.920 --> 01:32.100
Yeah.

01:32.180 --> 01:35.540
Because the core mission today isn't just to gawk at what AI gets wrong.

01:35.540 --> 01:44.340
We're looking at why our deepest assumptions regarding how these models store information are fundamentally and perhaps even mathematically flawed.

01:45.140 --> 01:49.140
Right. Because the vocabulary we use dictates how we troubleshoot the systems we build.

01:49.540 --> 01:55.100
When developers or even just everyday end users, when they see an AI fail, they immediately reach for hardware metaphors.

01:55.180 --> 01:58.220
Oh, totally. Like, oh, it hallucinated. It must have a corrupted memory bank.

01:58.220 --> 02:03.200
Exactly. They say the model lost the fact or the data isn't stored correctly.

02:03.580 --> 02:07.080
But this paper argues those metaphors are not just imprecise.

02:07.180 --> 02:12.560
They're actually leading the entire field of AI research into a total blind alley.

02:12.760 --> 02:15.540
We all talk about AI hallucinating or forgetting facts.

02:15.640 --> 02:21.320
But if this paper is saying we're using the wrong metaphor entirely, I mean, if it's not a memory bank, what is it?

02:21.720 --> 02:27.560
Well, to understand the new theory, we first have to look at the massive tests that exposed the problem in the first place.

02:27.560 --> 02:36.400
The entire paper kind of revolves around an anomaly that was exposed by this massive testing framework called the Wiki Profile Benchmark.

02:36.960 --> 02:41.700
And the scale of that benchmark is what caught my eye immediately when I was reading through the source material.

02:42.200 --> 02:47.040
I mean, we're not talking about a few cherry-picked examples of ChatGPT getting confused on Twitter.

02:47.360 --> 02:51.000
No, not at all. They ran this across 13 different frontier models.

02:51.200 --> 02:55.580
They generated something like 4.5 million distinct responses.

02:55.580 --> 02:57.460
Wow. Four and a half million.

02:57.560 --> 03:04.480
Yeah. And they were testing this highly curated set of about two 150 facts derived directly from Wikipedia.

03:04.920 --> 03:09.320
And the architecture of the test itself is split across two very specific axes.

03:09.780 --> 03:12.540
You have encoding and you have knowing.

03:13.180 --> 03:18.160
Right. And looking at the methodology, the encoding axis tests whether the model can reproduce a fact

03:18.160 --> 03:30.500
when given a strongly primed source-like prompt, basically a prompt that mirrors the exact contextual neighborhood where that fact lived in the original training data.

03:30.580 --> 03:33.220
It's the most ideal condition possible for the AI.

03:33.380 --> 03:37.320
Yeah. And under those ideal conditions, the models pass with staggering consistency.

03:37.320 --> 03:40.700
We're talking 95% to 98% success rates.

03:41.000 --> 03:41.060
Yeah.

03:41.100 --> 03:44.500
So they demonstrably possess the capability to output the fact.

03:44.580 --> 03:47.700
They possess the capability under highly privileged constraints, yes.

03:48.240 --> 03:52.900
But the friction really arises when the benchmark shifts to the second axis, which is the knowing test.

03:53.080 --> 03:54.580
Right. And this is where it gets brutal.

03:54.580 --> 03:58.080
Exactly. Here, the environment becomes actively hostile to the model.

03:58.180 --> 04:03.960
The AI has to reproduce that exact same fact, but under four minimally supported, differently constructed questions.

04:04.020 --> 04:05.460
So no more spoon feeding the context.

04:05.580 --> 04:10.060
Well, right. Two of the questions target the subject of the fact and two target the object.

04:10.580 --> 04:16.220
But crucially, to pass this knowing test, the model cannot just get one right and pass.

04:16.420 --> 04:19.080
It must succeed on all four variations.

04:19.080 --> 04:30.080
All four. And the failure rate there is just shocking. I mean, between 25% to 33% of facts that were perfectly in code in the first test completely fail this second test.

04:30.160 --> 04:31.720
Which is a massive drop off.

04:31.820 --> 04:41.520
Huge. And even if you give the model what they call inference time compute, like allowing it to think and generate tokens before answering, 11% to 12% still fail entirely.

04:41.960 --> 04:44.400
But I have to pause on the architecture of that test for a second.

04:44.480 --> 04:45.540
Sure. What stands out to you?

04:45.540 --> 04:56.160
Well, reading through the methodology, the gap between a 98% success rate and a 33% failure rate, it feels, I don't know, somewhat manufactured by the logic of the test itself.

04:56.280 --> 04:57.200
Ah, I see where you're going.

04:57.360 --> 04:59.420
Right. Because you have this massive structural imbalance.

04:59.980 --> 05:02.640
The encoding test is basically an existential quantifier.

05:02.780 --> 05:07.580
It's just asking, does there exist at least one highly tailored prompt that gets you to the right answer?

05:08.120 --> 05:10.560
If the model gets just one open door, it passes.

05:11.060 --> 05:14.040
It just needs a single success to prove the capability.

05:14.040 --> 05:18.560
Exactly. But then the knowing test is a universal quantifier.

05:19.120 --> 05:26.140
It's demanding, do all four of these difficult, weirdly phrased prompts lead to the right answer?

05:26.360 --> 05:29.200
It cannot have any closed doors at all.

05:29.280 --> 05:31.340
So it's mathematically rigged to be harder.

05:31.620 --> 05:37.580
Right. If you compare a highly supportive existential test against a minimally supportive universal test,

05:37.580 --> 05:43.360
aren't you just mathematically guaranteeing a massive gap just by virtue of how you set up the rules?

05:43.800 --> 05:47.560
That is a very sharp critique of the benchmark's underlying logic.

05:47.720 --> 05:49.460
And honestly, it raises an important question.

05:49.980 --> 05:52.580
But Flyxion actually addresses this directly in the paper.

05:53.140 --> 05:56.440
He also concedes that a significant portion of this gap is definitional.

05:56.660 --> 05:59.540
It's driven purely by that quantifier structure you just described.

05:59.540 --> 06:02.320
Okay, so they admit it's a bit of an apples to oranges comparison.

06:02.480 --> 06:05.260
Yes. Comparing any to all is always going to yield a delta.

06:05.680 --> 06:09.960
But the argument here is that we shouldn't just discard the benchmark because of that logical asymmetry.

06:10.440 --> 06:13.460
Instead, we need to focus on the absolute size of the gap.

06:13.900 --> 06:16.620
And more importantly, exactly which facts fall into it.

06:16.820 --> 06:21.620
Because 30% is still a huge chunk of information to just disappear.

06:21.620 --> 06:31.520
Exactly. When the benchmark creators saw this 30% drop-off, their immediate reaction was to explain it using the exact hardware metaphors we mentioned earlier.

06:32.220 --> 06:36.220
They used terms like empty shelves and lost keys in their own reporting.

06:36.600 --> 06:38.120
Empty shelves. Wow.

06:38.320 --> 06:41.780
Yeah, they theorized that the fact is structurally present in the parameters,

06:42.020 --> 06:47.000
but the model just couldn't locate the key to unlock that specific door when asked in a weird way.

06:47.100 --> 06:50.720
Which brings us to the core philosophical target of this paper, right?

06:50.720 --> 06:52.480
The container ontology.

06:53.040 --> 06:53.740
That's the heart of it.

06:53.880 --> 06:58.760
Because our entire mental map of computing is just so deeply biased by the von Neumann architecture.

06:59.200 --> 07:05.060
We are so used to the idea of a central processing unit pulling discrete files from a physical hard drive.

07:05.160 --> 07:07.320
It's how every computer we've ever used works.

07:07.540 --> 07:11.880
Right. And it's incredibly difficult for us not to project that architecture onto a neural network.

07:11.880 --> 07:18.200
We assume a fact, like Paris is the capital of France, is a discrete addressable object.

07:18.200 --> 07:23.360
Like a box sitting in a specific multidimensional location inside the AI's parameters.

07:23.860 --> 07:27.860
And if you buy into that container idea, then a prompt is just a memory address.

07:27.980 --> 07:31.500
Exactly. A good prompt points directly to the shelf.

07:31.900 --> 07:35.440
A bad prompt is just a corrupted map that leads you to the wrong aisle.

07:35.840 --> 07:38.060
And that is the container ontology in a nutshell.

07:38.580 --> 07:41.100
It treats factual competence as a state of possession.

07:41.720 --> 07:44.180
The model either has the fact or it doesn't.

07:44.180 --> 07:47.320
But Flyxion points out a fatal flaw in this reasoning.

07:47.940 --> 07:49.760
We never actually see the container.

07:50.460 --> 07:52.360
Right. We can't look inside the black box.

07:52.700 --> 07:57.780
Exactly. When we run a benchmark, we are purely observing the model's behavioral output.

07:58.240 --> 08:01.700
We feed it a string of text, and we observe the string of text it returns.

08:02.360 --> 08:05.840
We do not have an x-ray machine that looks into the billions of parameters

08:05.840 --> 08:10.240
and verifies the existence of a discrete fact object sitting on a shelf.

08:10.380 --> 08:11.900
So the container is an illusion.

08:11.900 --> 08:16.600
It is an inference we project it onto the model to make its behavior intelligible to us.

08:17.300 --> 08:20.480
Flyxion argues that if we discard that container inference entirely

08:20.480 --> 08:24.160
and replace it with something called a residue and trajectory ontology,

08:24.380 --> 08:28.860
the erratic behavior of these models suddenly aligns perfectly with the underlying math.

08:29.260 --> 08:30.660
Okay. Residue and trajectory.

08:31.120 --> 08:36.820
So instead of thinking of the AI's brain as a giant warehouse full of boxes on shelves,

08:37.340 --> 08:40.640
we should think of it like a wilderness territory.

08:40.640 --> 08:42.160
I love this analogy.

08:42.540 --> 08:47.620
Yeah, because if the parameter space is a vast, dense, untouched wilderness,

08:48.120 --> 08:53.720
then the training text, all those trillions of tokens the AI reads, that's like foot traffic.

08:54.060 --> 08:56.180
Millions of hikers walking through the woods.

08:56.460 --> 08:56.860
Exactly.

08:57.320 --> 09:00.240
The fact isn't a treasure chest hidden somewhere in the woods.

09:00.380 --> 09:02.340
It's a reinforced hiking trail.

09:02.820 --> 09:06.640
It's literally built by the historical traffic of the training data.

09:06.640 --> 09:13.260
Generating an answer is just walking down a legally admissible path from wherever the tromp dropped you off.

09:13.320 --> 09:16.800
Which completely redefines what a fact actually is in the context of AI.

09:17.060 --> 09:19.700
Yeah, the parameters are just the dirt, the trampled dirt.

09:19.840 --> 09:24.240
Right. And a fact is a deeply grooved, highly reinforced trail in that dirt.

09:24.360 --> 09:28.920
It formed because that specific sequence of tokens was walked repeatedly during training.

09:28.920 --> 09:33.520
So when you prompt the model, you aren't sending a retrieval query to a database.

09:33.960 --> 09:35.700
You're just dropping a new hiker into the woods.

09:36.080 --> 09:39.820
Precisely. You take the prompt, drop it somewhere in this high-dimensional wilderness,

09:40.220 --> 09:45.340
and watch it walk down whatever path naturally unfolds in front of it based on the contours of the ground.

09:45.740 --> 09:47.440
Okay. This is clicking for me now.

09:48.020 --> 09:51.800
If you apply that wilderness analogy back to the wiki profile benchmark,

09:52.300 --> 09:55.240
that massive performance gap isn't a mystery at all.

09:55.240 --> 09:57.960
Not at all. Think about the encoding test.

09:58.460 --> 10:03.440
A strongly primed, source-like prompt doesn't open a door to a stored object.

10:03.560 --> 10:08.240
It just drops the hiker exactly at the trailhead of a heavily paved, highly trafficked road.

10:08.540 --> 10:13.920
Exactly. It simply reconstructs the exact conditions under which a specific trajectory

10:13.920 --> 10:15.900
was originally reinforced during training.

10:16.480 --> 10:19.260
Of course, the model succeeds 98% of the time.

10:19.400 --> 10:20.740
Because it's an easy walk.

10:20.740 --> 10:27.320
But that success only proves that one specific path exists from one specific starting coordinate.

10:27.920 --> 10:32.340
It absolutely does not prove the existence of a universal omnidirectional fact object.

10:32.420 --> 10:35.620
Right. And then the benchmark pivots to the knowing test.

10:35.760 --> 10:39.280
Right. Where it takes that same hiker and drops him in a dense thicket

10:39.280 --> 10:43.120
three miles off the main trail facing the wrong direction with zero signage.

10:43.120 --> 10:48.420
And when the model inevitably outputs a hallucination or fails to reach the destination,

10:49.000 --> 10:51.820
the container ontology observers throw up their hands and say,

10:52.240 --> 10:55.060
oh no, the shelf is empty, the fact has vanished.

10:55.240 --> 10:56.560
But there was never a shelf.

10:56.740 --> 10:56.900
Yeah.

10:57.180 --> 11:01.280
You just dropped the system in a location where no historical foot traffic

11:01.280 --> 11:03.240
had ever carved a path to the target.

11:03.680 --> 11:04.680
That makes so much sense.

11:05.000 --> 11:05.780
It really does.

11:05.900 --> 11:09.160
And this hiking trail theory, this trajectory framework,

11:09.160 --> 11:15.020
becomes incredibly predictive when we look at the specific glitches found in the benchmark data.

11:15.220 --> 11:16.540
Which brings us to the anomalies.

11:16.700 --> 11:16.940
Yes.

11:17.460 --> 11:22.340
The researchers identified two major conditions that almost guarantee a fact will fall into this gap,

11:22.640 --> 11:27.140
meaning it passes the easy encoding test, but fails the robust knowing test.

11:27.640 --> 11:31.260
Those conditions are reverse phrasing and low popularity in the training data.

11:31.260 --> 11:35.520
Okay, let's dig into reverse phrasing first, because the reversal curse is,

11:35.840 --> 11:38.240
honestly, it's one of the most frustrating things to deal with

11:38.240 --> 11:41.520
when you are actually building applications on top of these LLMs.

11:41.680 --> 11:43.000
It's a huge headache for developers.

11:43.380 --> 11:43.620
Yeah.

11:43.880 --> 11:47.160
The paper highlights this using a specific example with the band Oasis.

11:48.040 --> 11:53.480
Under a container theory, the fact the band whose first gig was at the boardwalk was Oasis,

11:53.940 --> 11:57.180
and the phrase Oasis played its first gig at the boardwalk,

11:57.180 --> 12:01.860
are the exact same logical object, subject, verb, object.

12:02.200 --> 12:04.020
Logically, yes, they are identical.

12:04.220 --> 12:04.400
Right.

12:04.520 --> 12:07.000
So if that relationship is stored in a discrete container,

12:07.480 --> 12:10.760
the model should be able to query it from either direction effortlessly.

12:11.580 --> 12:16.380
Under a storage ontology, failing the reverse question looks like the AI is having a stroke.

12:16.960 --> 12:19.760
The same fact is present and then mysteriously absent.

12:20.160 --> 12:22.940
But under our hiking trail theory, it makes perfect sense.

12:23.660 --> 12:24.120
Tell me why.

12:24.120 --> 12:28.020
Well, what's fascinating here is the mechanics of autoregressive training.

12:28.420 --> 12:31.340
We have to remember that these models aren't just broadly reading text.

12:31.620 --> 12:34.980
They are strictly bound by left-to-right causal masking.

12:35.380 --> 12:38.380
Meaning they only predict the next word based on the previous words.

12:38.660 --> 12:39.080
Exactly.

12:39.360 --> 12:40.960
They build directional continuations.

12:41.500 --> 12:45.460
And in natural English, we almost always introduce the familiar entity first.

12:45.640 --> 12:50.400
The training data is absolutely saturated with the forward sequence Oasis played at the boardwalk.

12:50.400 --> 12:56.340
So the gradient updates during training heavily bias the vector weights to support that forward transition.

12:56.760 --> 12:56.880
Right.

12:57.200 --> 13:00.360
The forward direction was walked and reinforced millions of times.

13:00.580 --> 13:08.120
But the reverse direction, the band whose first gig was at the boardwalk was Oasis, has almost no direct support.

13:08.480 --> 13:09.760
Syntactically, it's rare.

13:09.960 --> 13:11.400
It has to be assembled from scratch.

13:11.540 --> 13:13.400
They are not the same trajectory at all.

13:13.540 --> 13:13.820
Right.

13:13.820 --> 13:16.240
The forward path is a paved highway.

13:16.780 --> 13:18.880
The reverse path is untouched brush.

13:19.320 --> 13:27.220
So the model fails the reverse career because it literally lacks the vector weights, the trampled dirt, to support that specific directional transition.

13:27.460 --> 13:30.640
And that logic scales perfectly to the second anomaly too, right?

13:30.700 --> 13:31.580
The long tail effect.

13:31.660 --> 13:31.920
Yes.

13:32.360 --> 13:35.680
Similarly, low popularity facts leave a much thinner residue.

13:36.240 --> 13:42.320
If a fact is only mentioned a handful of times across trillions of tokens, it resides in a very sparse data manifold.

13:42.320 --> 13:43.980
The trail is incredibly faint.

13:44.420 --> 13:46.260
Like the grass is barely pressed down.

13:46.600 --> 13:47.040
Exactly.

13:47.680 --> 13:52.180
A minimally supportive prompt is far less likely to land on one of these faint paths.

13:52.780 --> 13:55.300
Even a slight deviation means you will never find it.

13:55.660 --> 14:04.180
But a strongly supportive prompt, which drops you right at the trailhead, can still navigate it because it places you directly on the few footprints that exist.

14:04.540 --> 14:08.380
That is profound for anyone relying on AI for research.

14:08.380 --> 14:12.600
The popularity of a fact isn't about whether it's stored or not.

14:12.980 --> 14:17.280
It's just a proxy for how many contextual entrances exist to that path.

14:17.660 --> 14:18.000
Precisely.

14:18.400 --> 14:20.720
Which naturally leads us to a massive question.

14:21.300 --> 14:24.440
What happens when the AI is given time to think before answering?

14:24.740 --> 14:25.880
Does that rebuild the path?

14:25.880 --> 14:36.680
Yeah, because we are seeing models now, like OpenAI's 01 series, that are explicitly designed to generate this internal chain of thought before they spit out a final answer.

14:36.820 --> 14:39.060
And the wiki profile researchers actually tested this.

14:39.380 --> 14:45.440
They allowed models to have inference time computation, giving them space to reason before answering the difficult knowing prompts.

14:45.540 --> 14:46.100
What did they find?

14:46.100 --> 14:50.980
They found that this intermediate computation recovered a huge fraction of the facts.

14:51.540 --> 14:57.320
Somewhere between 40 to 65 percent of the facts that failed direct recall suddenly became successes.

14:58.020 --> 14:58.360
Wow.

14:58.700 --> 14:59.480
Over half.

14:59.880 --> 15:03.060
And I'm guessing the original paper called this a recall mechanism, right?

15:03.120 --> 15:05.300
Because they're stuck in the container mindset.

15:05.700 --> 15:06.160
Exactly.

15:06.380 --> 15:11.280
They saw it as the model taking extra time to search the warehouse and find the lost key on the shelf.

15:11.780 --> 15:14.640
But Flyxion completely deconstructs this assumption.

15:14.640 --> 15:20.460
He breaks down why labeling this as a single recall mechanism is deeply ambiguous and misleading.

15:20.880 --> 15:23.300
Because it's actually doing different things behind the scenes.

15:23.400 --> 15:23.580
Right.

15:23.840 --> 15:29.380
The single category of recall with thinking likely contains three entirely different operations.

15:29.780 --> 15:31.700
The first one is what he calls repair.

15:31.900 --> 15:32.140
Okay.

15:32.340 --> 15:32.660
Repair.

15:32.720 --> 15:33.320
What does that look like?

15:33.480 --> 15:39.400
Repair is reconstructing the missing context to reconnect a poor prompt to an already reinforced trajectory.

15:39.400 --> 15:47.240
If a prompt drops the model slightly off the faint trail, the intermediate computation allows it to generate its own contextual stepping stones.

15:47.740 --> 15:51.440
It slowly steers its internal states back toward the paved highway.

15:51.720 --> 15:51.960
Okay.

15:52.060 --> 15:59.240
So if I use our analogy, repair is like bushwhacking your way until you find the main hiking trail again.

15:59.360 --> 16:00.280
Perfect way to put it.

16:00.280 --> 16:02.560
But then there's the second operation, which is reconstruction.

16:03.280 --> 16:04.700
And that sounds different.

16:04.940 --> 16:05.600
Very different.

16:05.720 --> 16:11.520
In reconstruction, the model derives the correct answer from completely different related propositions.

16:11.800 --> 16:14.340
It takes a totally different route to the same destination.

16:14.620 --> 16:16.540
So it never actually finds the original trail.

16:16.720 --> 16:17.040
Never.

16:17.400 --> 16:23.520
It uses secondary facts or deductive reasoning that just mathematically converges on the right output.

16:23.960 --> 16:24.180
Okay.

16:24.220 --> 16:28.560
So reconstruction is like calling an Uber to get to the same destination instead of walking the trail.

16:28.620 --> 16:29.020
Yes.

16:29.020 --> 16:30.320
But here's the catch.

16:30.860 --> 16:33.240
How do we know which one the AI is actually doing?

16:34.060 --> 16:38.420
If I'm just looking at the final answer, how do I know if it bushwhacked or called an Uber?

16:38.860 --> 16:40.600
That's the million dollar question.

16:41.020 --> 16:46.060
And Flyxion's point is that the behavioral evidence in the benchmark simply cannot distinguish between the two.

16:46.900 --> 16:52.480
Seeing the AI get the right answer after thinking does not prove it repaired, a stored fact.

16:52.680 --> 16:55.940
It might have just mathematically reconstructed it via a side door.

16:55.940 --> 16:57.660
The test is blind to the mechanism.

16:57.660 --> 16:59.980
And wait, you mentioned a third operation.

17:00.300 --> 17:01.380
Yes, local search.

17:01.820 --> 17:09.040
This happens because the benchmark proves that for autoregressive models, recognition is vastly easier than generation.

17:09.300 --> 17:09.500
Right.

17:09.620 --> 17:12.260
Evaluating options is easier than coming up with them from scratch.

17:12.260 --> 17:12.680
Exactly.

17:13.080 --> 17:16.640
So during local search, the model isn't bushwhacking or Ubering.

17:16.880 --> 17:20.680
It is generating an internal multiple choice test for itself.

17:21.040 --> 17:30.500
It hallucinates three or four highly probable candidate strings and then uses its internal discriminator capabilities to evaluate which one has the highest relative likelihood.

17:30.500 --> 17:37.020
Oh, wow. So it just makes up options A, D, C, and D, and then realizes B feels the most mathematically sound.

17:37.300 --> 17:43.920
Yes. And again, to the observer, all three of these, repair, reconstruction, and local search look identical.

17:44.280 --> 17:46.960
They all result in a correct final string of text.

17:46.960 --> 17:51.660
So the benchmark creators lump them all together and say, look, it recalled the fact from storage.

17:52.380 --> 17:53.080
But it didn't.

17:53.580 --> 18:01.840
This reliance on purely behavioral observation, just grading the final text, it leads to another massive flaw in how we judge AI, doesn't it?

18:02.060 --> 18:02.820
It really does.

18:03.040 --> 18:08.960
It leads to what we can call the sampling trap, which is a major critique of how we grade these AI failures.

18:09.100 --> 18:09.980
The sampling trap.

18:10.340 --> 18:12.760
Okay, how did the benchmark actually grade the questions?

18:12.760 --> 18:16.380
In the benchmark, each question is sampled eight times.

18:16.960 --> 18:19.040
They give the model an eight-attempt budget.

18:19.740 --> 18:25.840
If it fails to produce the exact correct string on all eight attempts, it is marked as completely inaccessible.

18:26.320 --> 18:28.860
Which, if you are testing a human, makes sense.

18:29.360 --> 18:33.080
If I ask you a trivia question eight times and you get it wrong eight times, you don't know it.

18:33.260 --> 18:36.800
Right. But an AI doesn't operate in true-false binaries.

18:37.000 --> 18:39.640
It operates on continuous probability distributions.

18:39.640 --> 18:48.040
Flyxion explains that by marking it inaccessible, the benchmark quietly converts a continuous probability into a binary pass-fail.

18:48.180 --> 18:50.900
So they're forcing a binary on a mathematical spectrum.

18:51.160 --> 18:51.600
Exactly.

18:51.900 --> 18:57.360
If the mathematical probability of outputting the correct answer for a specific prompt is small but non-zero,

18:57.840 --> 19:01.020
let's say the P of F given Q is much less than 1 Pozen.

19:01.380 --> 19:02.540
Maybe it's a 2% chance.

19:03.040 --> 19:04.960
Eight samples will frequently miss it.

19:04.960 --> 19:10.940
Because eight tries at a 2% chance still leaves you mathematically likely to fail all eight times.

19:11.220 --> 19:11.740
Exactly.

19:12.120 --> 19:16.420
But missing it doesn't mean the transition function assigns its zero weight.

19:16.580 --> 19:18.700
It is not excluded from the parameters.

19:18.800 --> 19:20.100
It is just weakly weighted.

19:20.400 --> 19:24.020
Here is where it gets really interesting for anyone building AI tools.

19:24.020 --> 19:32.300
Because treating a low probability path as a lost key groups together very different types of failures, right?

19:32.500 --> 19:34.900
It totally misdiagnoses the system.

19:35.440 --> 19:39.020
And to fix this, Flyxion outlines a new taxonomy of failure.

19:39.600 --> 19:41.820
Three distinct kinds of deficits.

19:42.060 --> 19:43.420
Okay, walk me through the three deficits.

19:43.840 --> 19:45.500
First is a structural deficit.

19:45.860 --> 19:47.580
This is genuine non-encoding.

19:47.580 --> 19:53.260
It means no admissible transition exists at all between the prompt and the answer anywhere in the parameters.

19:53.660 --> 19:55.640
So the true probability is essentially zero.

19:55.820 --> 19:56.980
The dirt was never walked on.

19:57.060 --> 19:58.060
No path exists.

19:58.260 --> 19:58.460
Right.

19:58.720 --> 20:00.700
No amount of prompting or thinking will get you there.

20:00.880 --> 20:03.480
But the second one is where the paradigm really shifts.

20:03.960 --> 20:05.080
The energetic deficit.

20:05.320 --> 20:06.160
Energetic deficit.

20:06.300 --> 20:06.740
What is that?

20:06.920 --> 20:12.820
This means the path does exist, but it's too computationally expensive to traverse without intermediate steps.

20:13.160 --> 20:14.660
The reversal curse is a great example.

20:14.660 --> 20:22.860
The model can theoretically output the reverse relationship, but it lacks the contextual runway to bridge the gap directly.

20:23.500 --> 20:25.060
It needs intermediate stepping stones.

20:25.460 --> 20:30.520
It needs computational energy-like chain-of-thought tokens to lower the activation barrier.

20:30.760 --> 20:31.200
Exactly.

20:31.680 --> 20:33.080
The knowledge isn't missing.

20:33.520 --> 20:38.240
The model is simply starved of the computational energy required to traverse the path.

20:38.440 --> 20:40.640
This connects so directly to daily use.

20:40.640 --> 20:46.960
If I'm using an AI and it fails my prompt, it might not lack the knowledge, which would be a structural deficit.

20:47.640 --> 20:52.760
I just might not be giving it the computational budget to traverse the path, which is an energetic deficit.

20:53.020 --> 20:53.280
Right.

20:53.380 --> 20:54.640
You don't need to teach it the fact.

20:54.700 --> 20:56.060
You need to let it reason out loud.

20:56.200 --> 20:56.280
Yeah.

20:56.280 --> 20:58.720
And that leads to the third mode, the kinetic deficit.

20:58.780 --> 20:59.720
Okay, kinetic deficit.

20:59.720 --> 21:10.100
This is when recovery is structurally possible and energetically feasible, but it's just too slow or requires more sampling budget than allowed by task constraints.

21:10.340 --> 21:12.860
Ah, like the 2% probability example.

21:13.200 --> 21:13.740
Exactly.

21:13.940 --> 21:20.880
If it needs 50 tries to hit that 2% chance, but your API limits it to one generation, it fails.

21:20.880 --> 21:25.260
But it's a failure of kinetic allowance, not structural knowledge.

21:25.740 --> 21:28.740
This completely reframes how we debug these systems.

21:29.400 --> 21:38.400
But to truly prove that this gap is about local pathways and not a broken database, the author actually translates this entire argument into a totally different language, don't they?

21:38.460 --> 21:38.780
They do.

21:38.860 --> 21:40.360
They move into high-level mathematics.

21:41.040 --> 21:45.120
Specifically, Section 10 of the paper uses topology and category theory.

21:45.120 --> 21:51.060
Okay, I want to reassure you listening right now, we are not going to get bogged down in impenetrable math jargon.

21:51.320 --> 21:54.940
But the map here is beautifully illustrative of the problem.

21:55.100 --> 21:55.900
It really is.

21:56.280 --> 22:01.800
Flyxion uses the concept of pre-sheaves and topology to explain the gap between encoding and knowing.

22:02.080 --> 22:03.780
All right, let's deconstruct the math gently.

22:04.180 --> 22:05.480
How do we visualize this?

22:05.700 --> 22:09.760
Think of the different possible prompts for a single fact as subsets covering a space.

22:09.760 --> 22:16.760
The encoding test, the easy one, basically asks if there is a successful answer over some single object in that space.

22:17.080 --> 22:18.700
It's an existential local claim.

22:19.020 --> 22:21.580
The paper calls it having one non-empty stock, right?

22:22.020 --> 22:22.580
Exactly.

22:22.860 --> 22:24.980
One localized point of success.

22:25.640 --> 22:30.400
But the knowing test asks for a global section over the whole cover.

22:30.760 --> 22:35.540
It demands that every stock must be non-empty and they must all be compatible with each other.

22:36.040 --> 22:38.620
Let me try a real-world analogy to translate this.

22:38.620 --> 22:41.020
It sounds exactly like having cell phone service.

22:41.080 --> 22:41.820
Oh, I like that.

22:41.900 --> 22:42.420
Let's hear it.

22:42.520 --> 22:42.720
Okay.

22:42.900 --> 22:48.860
The encoding test just asks if you have a signal while standing on one specific mountain peak.

22:49.140 --> 22:52.280
If you can make a call from that exact spot, you pass.

22:52.520 --> 22:53.740
You have a non-empty stock.

22:54.020 --> 23:00.580
But the knowing test asks if you have a continuous unbroken signal while driving your car across the entire state.

23:00.840 --> 23:02.300
That is the global section, yes.

23:02.360 --> 23:02.520
Right.

23:02.680 --> 23:06.220
And if you lose signal in a valley, it doesn't mean your phone is fundamentally broken.

23:06.680 --> 23:07.820
It's not a storage failure.

23:07.820 --> 23:09.040
The cell tower didn't vanish.

23:09.420 --> 23:13.180
It is just an ordinary failure of a local signal to extend globally.

23:13.880 --> 23:16.020
That analogy maps flawlessly to the mathematics.

23:16.700 --> 23:23.140
Dropping a call is exactly how a pre-sheaf can have non-empty stocks at isolated points, but no global section.

23:23.340 --> 23:25.120
There's nothing wrong with a mathematical object.

23:25.320 --> 23:26.540
It's just behaving locally.

23:26.920 --> 23:29.240
And Flyxion also touches on formal logic, right?

23:29.500 --> 23:30.800
The Curry-Howard correspondence.

23:30.800 --> 23:32.760
Yes, the typing judgment aspect.

23:32.760 --> 23:41.680
In formal logic, deriving an answer under a rich, source-like prompt is like solving an equation where you're handed most of the variables.

23:42.080 --> 23:43.140
It's an open term.

23:43.580 --> 23:46.120
It relies heavily on a favorable context.

23:46.260 --> 23:48.500
But the knowing test demands a closed term.

23:48.500 --> 23:49.300
Exactly.

23:49.780 --> 23:52.880
An answer that doesn't depend on a highly favorable context.

23:53.180 --> 23:54.500
It has to stand on its own.

23:55.080 --> 23:58.500
And logically, a local proof does not guarantee a global proof.

23:58.760 --> 24:03.640
Which leads to this crucial warning chain for anyone who relies on AI to produce facts.

24:04.120 --> 24:07.120
Because we conflate these mathematical realities all the time.

24:07.220 --> 24:07.700
We do.

24:08.120 --> 24:11.760
Flyxion details what he calls the short non-collapse chain.

24:12.260 --> 24:15.340
We must stop conflating four distinct operational states.

24:15.640 --> 24:16.440
Let's run through them.

24:16.580 --> 24:17.080
Step one.

24:17.080 --> 24:21.840
Step one is successful completion, meaning answering a prompt correctly once.

24:22.240 --> 24:23.840
But that does not entail step two.

24:24.100 --> 24:25.400
Which is a stored proposition.

24:26.100 --> 24:29.660
Just because it answered correctly doesn't mean there's a discrete fact sitting in a box.

24:29.880 --> 24:31.520
It just means a path was walked.

24:32.100 --> 24:33.380
And that doesn't entail step three.

24:33.700 --> 24:33.980
Right.

24:34.820 --> 24:36.720
Step three is robust knowledge.

24:37.360 --> 24:40.900
Being reachable from unprivileged random starting points.

24:41.460 --> 24:46.460
A stored path might exist, but it might be inaccessible due to energetic deficits.

24:46.460 --> 24:46.920
Yeah.

24:47.080 --> 24:51.120
And even if it has robust knowledge, it does not entail step four.

24:51.120 --> 24:52.960
Step four is warranted commitment.

24:53.760 --> 24:57.040
A claim the system stands behind independent of the questioner framing.

24:57.040 --> 24:59.920
And language models cannot make warranted commitments.

24:59.920 --> 25:01.620
They just simulate continuations.

25:01.620 --> 25:03.580
So what does this all mean for the listener?

25:03.580 --> 25:12.060
Just because an AI spits out a fact under a perfectly engineered, heavily primed prompt, it does not mean the system has robust knowledge of that fact.

25:12.060 --> 25:15.060
And it certainly doesn't mean it will defend it under cross-examination.

25:15.060 --> 25:22.300
If we connect this to the bigger picture, we have to stop treating a momentary factual output from an LLM as a verified database query.

25:22.900 --> 25:25.060
Factual competence is not a state the model possesses.

25:25.740 --> 25:26.840
It's an active property.

25:27.020 --> 25:31.760
It's a real-time performance of whether historically formed distinctions can be carried forward into outputs.

25:31.760 --> 25:32.360
Exactly.

25:32.880 --> 25:35.520
But this leads to the ultimate paradox of the paper.

25:36.080 --> 25:41.240
We spent this whole time analyzing behavioral tests, just talking to the model and grading its answers.

25:41.500 --> 25:41.760
Right.

25:42.140 --> 25:45.960
And as we established, behavior can't prove if it bushwhacked or took an Uber.

25:45.960 --> 25:53.880
So if behavioral tests can never truly prove whether a fact is stored or just reconstructed, how will we ever know for sure?

25:54.440 --> 25:57.060
That is the final blow Felixion delivers.

25:57.780 --> 26:03.080
Throughout this deep dive, we've looked at behavior, but the paper outlines what actual proof would look like.

26:03.560 --> 26:04.960
We need an interventionist program.

26:05.720 --> 26:06.580
Meaning what exactly?

26:06.580 --> 26:16.520
Instead of varying the prompt on the outside, what if we directly perturbed or suppressed the internal representations, the actual neural activations inside the model while it was running?

26:16.880 --> 26:18.360
Like digital neurosurgery.

26:18.440 --> 26:18.800
Yes.

26:19.240 --> 26:30.400
If we could turn off a specific cluster of parameters and watch a fact selectively disappear across all conditions and then turn it back on and watch the fact instantly recover globally.

26:30.600 --> 26:34.540
Only then could we separate genuine repair from mathematical reconstruction.

26:34.540 --> 26:36.760
We would be mapping the literal paths.

26:37.160 --> 26:40.300
We would finally replace philosophical inference with empirical measurement.

26:40.680 --> 26:43.260
I want to leave you, the listener, with this thought to mull over.

26:43.880 --> 26:49.940
The future of understanding AI isn't going to look like psychology, where we just ask it questions and grade the answers.

26:50.480 --> 26:56.000
It's going to look like digital neuroscience, mapping the literal high-dimensional paths of its artificial brain.

26:56.400 --> 26:57.960
We have to find the hiking trails.

26:57.960 --> 27:04.640
Until we have those maps, remember that every answer an AI gives you is just a fragile path through a vast wilderness.

27:05.320 --> 27:07.080
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

27:07.440 --> 27:10.620
Keep exploring the trails of your own knowledge, and we'll see you next time.

